<article xml:id="week12-1" label="0012"> <!-- Change label to a random string of numbers for each worksheet. The label shows up in the url of the website and keeps students from searching for other worksheets -->
  <title>MATH 151 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
     For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!--START TYPING CONTENT FOR SESSION BELOW HERE-->
  <worksheet>
    <title> Session #12 (Final Exam Review, Part 1)</title>
    <p>
      Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you.
    </p>
    <paragraphs> <!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Getting Warmed Up and in the Right Mindset</title>
      <exercise><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider vectors <m>\mathbf{a}=2\mathbf{i}-3\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}=-\mathbf{i}+4\mathbf{j}</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the scalar (dot) product between <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>, <m>\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}=-14</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the scalar projection of <m>\mathbf{b}</m> onto <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>\text{comp}_{\mathbf{a}}(\mathbf{b})</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\comp_{\mathbf{a}}(\mathbf{(b)})=\dfrac{-14\sqrt{13}}{13}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the orthogonal, or vector, projection of <m>\mathbf{b}</m> onto <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf{a}}(\mathbf{b})</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf{a}}(\mathbf{b})=\left\langle -\dfrac{28}{13},\dfrac{42}{13}\right\rangle</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the angle between <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>, <m>\theta</m>. You may leave your answer in terms of inverse trigonometric functions, if applicable.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\theta=\arccos\left(-\dfrac{14}{\sqrt{13}\sqrt{17}}\right)</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Suppose that a box has been pulled <m>20</m>m up a <m>30^\circ</m> incline by a rope with a force <m>\mathbf{F}</m> wth magnitude <m>|\mathbf{F}|=120</m>N applied <m>30^\circ</m> counterclockwise from the direction of motion, as shown in the figure below.
              IMAGE
              How much work does the rope do on the box?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>1200\sqrt{3}</m>J
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Suppose that two forces act on an object. The first force, <m>\mathbf{F}_1</m>, has a magnitude of <m>20</m>N at an angle of <m>30^\circ</m> counterclockwise from the <m>x</m>-axis.
          The second force, <m>\mathbf{F}_2</m>, has a magnitude of <m>15</m>N at an angle of <m>135^\circ</m> counterclockwise from the <m>x</m>-axis.
          You may leave your answers to the following questions in terms fo unsimplified square roots and inverse trigonometric functions, if applicable.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine the magnitude of the resultant force.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>|\mathbf{F}_{net}|=\sqrt{\left(10\sqrt{3}-\dfrac{15\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)^2+\left(10+\dfrac{15\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)^2}\approx 21.67</m>N
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine the direction of the resultant force.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\theta=\arctan\left(\dfrac{10+\frac{15\sqrt{2}}{2}}{10\sqrt{3}-\frac{15\sqrt{2}}{2}}\right)\approx 71.95^\circ</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let <m>f(x)=\sqrt{1-3x}</m>. Use may not use derivative rules on Exercise 3.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Using the limit definition of the derivative, find <m>f'(x)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>f'(x)=-\dfrac{3}{2\sqrt{1-3x}}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the equation of the line tangent to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=5</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y-4=-\dfrac{3}{8}(x+5)</m> or <m>y=-\dfrac{3}{8}x+\dfrac{17}{8}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Find the derivative <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}</m> for each of the following functions. You may use derivative rules for this exercise.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=\sqrt[5]{2-9\sec(x)}\left(x^3-2\cot(x)\right)^{-\frac{2}{3}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\dfrac{1}{5}(2-9\sec(x))(-9\sec(x)\tan(x))\left(x^3-2\cot(x)\right)^{-\frac{2}{3}}-\dfrac{2}{3}\sqrt[5]{2-9\sec(x)}\left(x^3-2\cot(x)\right)^{-\frac{5}{3}}\left(3x^2+2\csc^2(x)\right)</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=-\dfrac{\csc(2x)}{5^{x^2}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\dfrac{5^{x^2}(-2\csc(2x)\cot(2x))-\csc(2x)5^{x^2}\ln(5)(2x)}{\left(5^{x^2}\right)^2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=\sin(\ln(\arcsin(-x)))</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\cos(\ln(\arcsin(-x)))\left(\dfrac{1}{\arcsin(-x)}\right)\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\right)(-1)</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=\begin{cases}\log_7(-\pi x) \amp x \lt 0 \\ e^x\cos\left(\sqrt{x}\right) \amp x \geq 0 \end{cases}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y=\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{x\ln(7)} \amp x \lt 0 \\ e^x\cos\left(\sqrt{x}\right)-e^x\dfrac{\sin\left(\sqrt{x}\right)}{2\sqrt{x}} \amp x \gt 0 \end{cases}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>y=(\tan(x))^{\arctan(x)}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(\tan(x))^{\arctan(x)}\left(\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\ln(\tan(x))+\arctan(x)\dfrac{\sec^2(x)}{\tan(x)}\right)</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the values of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> that make the function <m>g(x)=\begin{cases}Ax^2+B+1 \amp x\lt 2 \\ Bx-A-1 \amp x\geq 2 \end{cases}</m> continuous and differentiable for all real values of <m>x</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>A=-2</m>, <m>B=-8</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 3  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Now Grades Go Up</title>
      
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>\mathbf{a}=-11\mathbf{i}+13\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{b}=5\mathbf{i}-7\mathbf{j}</m>, <m>\mathbf{c}=-2\mathbf{i}+3\mathbf{j}</m>, find <m>\mathbf{a}-2\mathbf{b}-3\mathbf{c}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-27\mathbf{i}+36\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-15\mathbf{i}+18\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-7\mathbf{i}+8\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>5\mathbf{i}-10\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>7\mathbf{i}-8\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>A=(-1,-4)</m> and <m>B=(4,8)</m>, find a unit vector perpendicular to <m>\overrightarrow{BA}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle \dfrac{4}{5}, \dfrac{3}{5}\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle -\dfrac{3}{5}, \dfrac{4}{5}\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle \dfrac{5}{13}, -\dfrac{12}{13}\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle -\dfrac{12}{13}, \dfrac{5}{13}\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle 1, 0\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\sec\left(\arcsin\left(\dfrac{1}{3}x\right)\right)</m> to an algebraic expression involving <m>x</m> only.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{3}{x}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{\sqrt{9-x^2}}{x}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{9-x^2}}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{\sqrt{9-x^2}}{3}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{x}{\sqrt{9-x^2}}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (C)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the exact value of the cosine of the angle between the vectors <m>\langle -10,24\rangle</m> and <m>\langle 15,8 \rangle</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{21}{221}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{21}{221}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{1}{2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the shortest distance from the point <m>(3,2)</m> to the line <m>x+y=2</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{3\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{2\sqrt{2}}{3}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{2\sqrt{3}}{3}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{2\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                You now know two methods to solve this problem, either using vector projections or optimization. You may use either approach to solve this problem, as they both yield the same answer.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (A)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Describe the motion of a particle whose motion is subject to the function <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle -2+3\sin(t),-2-4\cos(t)\rangle</m> as <m>t</m> increases.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Clockwise around a circle.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Clockwise around a circle.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Clockwise around an ellipse.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Counterclockwise around an ellipse.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  None of these.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the vector equation for a line parallel to <m>\langle -6,1 \rangle</m> passing through the point <m>(2,3)</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\langle 3-6t, t+2 \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\langle 2-t, 6t+3 \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\langle -6+t, 2t+3 \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\langle 3-t, 6t+2 \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\langle 2-6t, t+3 \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (E)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="8"><!-- #8 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Eliminate the parameter <m>t</m> and simplify the parametric function <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle 5-t,t^2-2t+3 \rangle</m> to Cartesian form.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=x^2-8x-18</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=x^2-8x+18</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=x^2+12x+18</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=-x^2-8x+18</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>y=-x^2-8x-18</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="9"><!-- #9 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Using the graph below, determine the <m>a</m>-values where <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow a}f(x)</m> exists.
            IMAGE
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a=-5,-4,-3,0,1,4,7</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a=-4,-3,0,1,4</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a=-4,0,1</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a=-4,1</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>a=-4</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (C)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="10"><!-- #10 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 1}\dfrac{x^2-1}{x^2+x}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>1</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-1</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (A)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="11"><!-- #11 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 4}\dfrac{2x^2-4x-16}{x^3-7x^2+12x}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="12"><!-- #12 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 9} \dfrac{\sqrt{x}-3}{9-x}</m>
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{1}{6}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{1}{6}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{1}{9}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{1}{3}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{1}{3}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="13"><!-- #13 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 7^-}\dfrac{49-x^2}{|x-7|}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-14</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>14</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="14"><!-- #14 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}2-x^2\cos\left(-\dfrac{3}{x^2}\right)</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-1</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>1</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>2</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="15"><!-- #15 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            According to the Intermediate Value Theorem, which of the following intervals could contain a solution to the equation <m>x^4+3x-4=0</m>?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(-3,-2)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(-2,-1)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(-1,0)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(0,1)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(1,2)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
    
      <exercise number="16"><!-- #16 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\arctan\left(\dfrac{4x^2+x-3x^4}{-7x^3+2x+9}\right)</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>0</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\infty</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="17"><!-- #17 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow -\infty}\dfrac{6e^{-x}-e^{2x}}{-3e^{-x}+6e^{2x}}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-2</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{1}{6}</m>
